{"name":"Fading Torch","hex":"#f69a54","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fading","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of fade."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process by which something fades; gradual diminishment.","An Irish dance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fading"},{"term":"torch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A stick of wood or plant fibres twisted together, with one end soaked in a flammable substance such as resin or tallow and set on fire, which is held in the hand, put into a wall bracket, or stuck into the ground, and used chiefly as a light source.","A similarly shaped implement with a replaceable supply of flammable material; specifically, a pole with a lamp at one end."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To illuminate or provide (a place) with torches (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1).","To make damaging claims about (someone or something); to ruin the reputation of (someone or something); to disparage, to insult."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/torch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fading","blocks":[{"text":"In wireless communications, fading is the variation of signal attenuation over variables like time, geographical position, and radio frequency. Fading is often modeled as a random process. In wireless systems, fading may either be due to multipath propagation, referred to as multipath-induced fading, weather, or shadowing from obstacles affecting the wave propagation, sometimes referred to as shadow fading."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/330px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fading"},{"title":"Torch","blocks":[{"text":"A torch is a stick with combustible material at one end which can be used as a light source or to set something on fire. Torches have been used throughout history and are still used in processions, symbolic and religious events, and in juggling and entertainment. In some countries, notably the United Kingdom and Australia, \"torch\" in modern usage is also the term for a battery-operated portable light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Lewes_Bonfire%2C_discarded_torch.jpg/330px-Lewes_Bonfire%2C_discarded_torch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torch"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Radio_propagation_noise.jpg/500px-Radio_propagation_noise.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frequency-selective time-varying fading causes a cloudy pattern to appear on a spectrogram. Time is shown on the horizontal axis, frequency on the vertical axis and signal strength as grey-scale intensity.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARadio_propagation_noise.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Coherence_time.png/500px-Coherence_time.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoherence_time.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/500px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Coherence_bandwidth.png/1280px-Coherence_bandwidth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_cam